  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday night...\n\nPatchy fog has developed across central Indiana this morning due to \nvery light winds and mostly clear skies. Current observations show \nthis fog has mostly developed in climatologically favored locations \nin valleys or near rivers. Look for this to become more common in \nfavored locations through daybreak before then quickly mixing out\nafter sunrise.\n \nMostly quiet weather conditions are expected early this week with \nsurface high pressure across the region. Aloft, upper troughing \nshifts east into Tuesday while ridging builds in. This should allow \nfor a slight moderation in temperatures, but seasonably cool \ntemperatures will persist given negligible thermal advection. Highs \nwill generally range from the mid 70s to low 80s.\n\nVery light winds and mostly clear skies again tonight into Tuesday \nmorning will promote patchy fog development. Any fog that develops \nshould quickly mix out in typical diurnal fashion. Surface high \npressure begins to shift east late Tuesday allowing for surface flow \nto become more southerly late overnight.\n\nWednesday through Sunday\n\nA weakening upper level low riding overtop of the western ridge will \ndrop into the upper Midwest early Wednesday before becoming absorbed \nby a broader long wave trough that will amplify across the eastern \nhalf of the country for late week. Low pressure and an associated \ncold front running in tandem with the upper wave will track across \nthe Ohio Valley Wednesday evening through early Thursday in a \nprogressively weakened condition as stronger forcing aloft is lost. \nThere will be enough moisture and instability along and immediately \nahead of the frontal boundary for scattered convection with the \nfront clearing the forecast area during the day Thursday. Isolated \nconvection is possible on Thursday across east-central IN before the \nfront moves out. \n\nA few strong storms cannot be ruled out Wednesday evening into \nWednesday night as guidance depicts moderate instability ahead of \nthe approaching front along with slight enhancement of mid-upper \nlevel flow from the approaching wave aloft. Confidence remains low \ndue to varying model solutions, but trends will continue to be \nmonitored. These details should become more clear once guidance is \nbetter aligned.  \n\nHigh pressure will reestablish in the wake of the frontal passage \ndrifting slowly east through the Great Lakes into Saturday. This \nbring another multi day stretch of dry weather with seasonable \ntemperatures as highs will focus largely in the low to mid 80s. The \nridge will flatten to the west over the weekend opening the \npossibility for convection to impact the region by late weekend into \nearly next week as multiple waves ride along in the predominantly \nzonal flow aloft. Temperatures will also rise back into the mid and \nupper 80s by the end of the forecast period with an uptick in \nhumidity levels.",
